A||||19||Jim Baumann||Motor Swaps||||5||
Z||000000||Jim Baumann||12-06-1999||06:11 PM||||Will a 3.1 out of a 94 95 Beretta or Corsica bolt straght into my 86 GT 4spd or are these the motors that need the starter relocated and a flange filed off?  Also if I can find a 3.1 from a gm van is it a straite trade or should the heads from the Fiero be put on any of these 3.1's.  I've been considering redoing my 2.8 into a 3.1 but the parts are going to run me around $2000 and take 2 months to get here where as I can get one of these 3.1 for $700 - $900.  My present motor has a blown head gasket and needs a clutch.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Monkeyman||12-06-1999||08:02 PM||||What about a 3.1 out of a '90 Beretta GT?  Will that work.  I can get one of those cheap and it sure would be nice to replace my 4 cyl with.  Every time I find a deal on a complete 2.8 somebody sells it out from under me.||||reg||5
Z||000002||william_thorniii||12-06-1999||08:37 PM||||Monkey.. the 3.1 from the L-4 will be a good upgrade.  However, to upgrade to a 3.1L from a 2.8L is useless imho.  The slight increase in bhp is not worth the $2000.  If you want to get bumped to around 200 bhp then go with a turbo unit.  Pre-fabbed the system will run around $2000, but can be done for about $700 if you go to the boneyard and research your project.  That will not only get you more bang for the buck, but better performance as well.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||3
Z||000003||batboy||12-06-1999||11:08 PM||||The 3.1 from a Corsica and other FWD cars have aluminum heads. While these are nice heads (that probably flow better than the factory Fiero heads), the problem is that the Fiero manifold will not fit on it, which means you need to install the ECM and wiring harness from the donor car too. The piston domes are shaped different too, so I don't know what trouble you'd get into swapping the Fiero heads onto it without changing pistons. The early 1990's 3.1 with cast iron heads from a minivan will work, but the heads have smaller valves and the cam is very mild. This is a good swap if you use a different cam and the Fiero heads and manifold. looks factory too. Ed Parks has a good article about swapping the 3.1 minivan engine in. My "links" page on my website has a link to Ed Parks' site. Several old Forum topics discuss this.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 12-06-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||12-07-1999||12:12 AM||||Let me ask my question in a different way.  If I have a car set up for a V6 (I don't but I will soon, God willing), can I just drop in the 3.1 ('90 Beretta) or do I have to modify something?||||reg||5
Z||000005||batboy||12-07-1999||02:19 AM||||If you mean will it bolt up to the tranny, then the answer is yes. But the Fiero computer and engine wiring harness will not work with it. You have to use the harness and computer (ECM) from the Beretta.||||reg||1
Z||000006||DaRkLoRD||12-07-1999||02:39 AM||||is it a lot of work to swap the wiring harness and computer?||||reg||1
Z||000007||dogtired||12-07-1999||12:56 PM||||Be very careful here! <BR>As I understand it several motors will bolt right up to our trannys but the starter is on the wrong side and it is difficult to drill them properly.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><BR>I almost bought a Camaro motor till I discovered that.||||reg||6
Z||000008||batboy||12-07-1999||03:25 PM||||The Camaro and other rear wheel drive vehicles usually do have the starter on the wrong side. I don't know if every single front wheel drive 60 degree V6 engine will fit, but most of them will. Another trouble area to watch with this swap is the front pulleys, belts, and accessories (such as alternator, A/C, etc.). You may have to use the Fiero V6 setup on the 3.1 engine.<P>I've never replaced the wiring harness, but have heard it's very time consuming. You might have to take parts of both and combine them. Like the engine wiring from the Beretta and the rear lighting wiring from the Fiero. Don't know, sounds like you might be opening up a can of worms. I'm sure it can be done, but probably a lot of work. I suspect it would be best to have the whole donor car available to work from.||||reg||1
Z||000009||william_thorniii||12-07-1999||06:18 PM||||Supposedly the engines from GM FWD minivans are set up correctly.  The most common swap right now is the 3.4L from a V-6 Camaro (Not the TDC).  Ed Parks has done probably a hundred of these in Fiero's.  He also sells them ready to drop in.  Monkeyman... you should check out this page <A HREF="http://aaron.koch.net/fiero.htm." TARGET=_blank>http://aaron.koch.net/fiero.htm.</A>   It belongs to Aaron Koch.  He dropped in a 3.4L prepared by Ed Parks.  In actuality the 3.4L is just another GM 60 degree pushrod V-6.  It is the only one worth swapping in.  The 3800 Buick engine would be another good swap.. but it is MUCH, MUCH more involved.  <P>William T. Thorn, III ||||reg||1
Z||000010||four50four||12-07-1999||06:24 PM||||try this...  <A HREF="http://aaron.koch.net/fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://aaron.koch.net/fiero.htm</A> <p>[This message has been edited by four50four (edited 12-07-1999).]||||reg||5
Z||000011||Phil||12-07-1999||06:24 PM||||The 3800 is a GREAT swap heheheheheh<BR>Better than a clunky ole V8 right Bat?<p>[This message has been edited by Phil (edited 12-07-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||batboy||12-07-1999||08:32 PM||||Well Phil, certainly more sophisticated, better gas mileage, and lighter weight. I'm guessing that right now, Phil's little 3800 supercharged gem could beat the Batmobile with it's current small block. But just wait until I get my new engine built and put in sometime during the next millennium.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I have all the machine work done to the block, crank, and rods. Lots of parts gathered up. I've sunk about $1000 into it so far and estimate about another $500 for the rest of the hydraulic roller valve train, plus about another $1000 for the aluminum high performance heads. Building a 400+ horsepower engine is not cheap and I'm doing all the manual labor myself. The engine alone is going to almost double what I got into the Batmobile so far (about $3000).||||reg||1
Z||000013||Phil||12-07-1999||09:23 PM||||Aha let the war begin!! Now where did I put that intercooler ?||||reg||1
Z||000014||Jim Baumann||12-08-1999||02:24 AM||||Is there any difference in using a 3.1 out of a GM minivan as a base for hopping up with a gm high perforance cam and headers or is it better to redo the 2.8 with the camero crank, Federal Mogul pistons as a base for trying to get around 200 hp.  I've been reading through Erics post of what he has in his 3.1 dated 10-21-99.  Or is the 3.4 now the way to go?  I do like the idea of keeping the motor looking stock with the fiero heads and manifold.||||reg||5
